--TEST--
A finished `Core\Compress` stream refuses a later `add` and a second `finish`, in both directions, and a `finish` that refused is finished too — spec § 17, `rule:core-api/a-lifetime-is-an-object`, `rule:core-classes/decompression-bound`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `finish` consumes the stream: a frame is whole once it has been answered, so
// there is nothing a later chunk could join and nothing a second `finish`
// could answer that would not be a second frame. Nothing in the accumulating
// implementation needs that rule — the chunks would still be there — and it is
// written anyway, because a coder is consumed by its own flush and a program
// that outgrows this shape should not have to change.
var $writing = Core\Compress::compressor(Core\Codec::Gzip);
$writing->add("the first half ");
$writing->add("and the second");
echo "frame: ", Core\Bytes::length($writing->finish()) > 0 ? "some octets" : "none", "\n";

try {
    $writing->add(" and a third");
    echo "a finished compressor took a chunk\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $late) {
    echo $late->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $writing->finish();
    echo "a finished compressor answered twice\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $again) {
    echo $again->message, "\n";
}

// The decompressing half is the same object rule under a different class, and
// the message names the class it was asked of rather than `Core\Compress`.
bytes $frame = Core\Compress::compress("something to decompress", Core\Codec::Zlib);
var $reading = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Zlib);
$reading->add($frame);
echo "octets: ", Core\Bytes::length($reading->finish()), "\n";
try {
    $reading->add($frame);
    echo "a finished decompressor took a chunk\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $more) {
    echo $more->message, "\n";
}

// And a `finish` that *refused* is finished as well: the stream releases its
// chunks before the decoder runs, so a bomb costs the ceiling once and a second
// attempt is a `RuntimeError` about a closed stream rather than a second
// `ParseError` about the same frame.
bytes $bomb = Core\Compress::compress(Core\Str::repeat("A", 16384), Core\Codec::Gzip);
var $refusing = Core\Compress::decompressor(Core\Codec::Gzip, 1024);
$refusing->add($bomb);
try {
    $refusing->finish();
    echo "the bound did not hold\n";
} catch (ParseError $over) {
    echo "refused: the whole stream's bound\n";
}
try {
    $refusing->finish();
    echo "a refused stream answered\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $closed) {
    echo $closed->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
frame: some octets
Core\Compress\Compressor::add(): this stream is finished — a frame is whole once it has been answered, so open a new stream with Core\Compress rather than reusing this one
Core\Compress\Compressor::finish(): this stream is finished — a frame is whole once it has been answered, so open a new stream with Core\Compress rather than reusing this one
octets: 23
Core\Compress\Decompressor::add(): this stream is finished — a frame is whole once it has been answered, so open a new stream with Core\Compress rather than reusing this one
refused: the whole stream's bound
Core\Compress\Decompressor::finish(): this stream is finished — a frame is whole once it has been answered, so open a new stream with Core\Compress rather than reusing this one
